VALUES ('Trustworthy social enterprises essential to combat greenwashing and pinkwashing', 'trustworthy-social-enterprises-essential-to-combat-greenwashing-and-pinkwashing', '<p><strong>Six winners shared €45,000 in prize money and they were:</strong></p>
<p>• <strong>The Shona Project</strong>, which supports girls aged 11-plus as they navigate the bullying, low self-esteem, and anxiety that have become far too prevalent a feature of the adolescent experience.</p>
<p><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2024/03/The-Shona-Project-was-founded-in-2016-by-Tammy-Darcy.png" alt="" width="1668" height="756" srcset="https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2024/03/The-Shona-Project-was-founded-in-2016-by-Tammy-Darcy.png 1668w, https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2024/03/The-Shona-Project-was-founded-in-2016-by-Tammy-Darcy-300x136.png 300w, https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2024/03/The-Shona-Project-was-founded-in-2016-by-Tammy-Darcy-1024x464.png 1024w, https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2024/03/The-Shona-Project-was-founded-in-2016-by-Tammy-Darcy-768x348.png 768w, https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2024/03/The-Shona-Project-was-founded-in-2016-by-Tammy-Darcy-1536x696.png 1536w, https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2024/03/The-Shona-Project-was-founded-in-2016-by-Tammy-Darcy-1080x489.png 1080w" sizes="(max-width: 1668px) 100vw, 1668px" /></p>
<p>• <strong>The Football Cooperative</strong>, whose recreational football brings adults together while promoting healthy ageing, positive mental health and community belonging.</p>
<p><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2024/03/The-Football-Cooperative-places-recreational-football-as-a-vehicle-to-promote-physical-activity-and-healthy-ageing-and-positive-mental-health-among-men.jpg" alt="" width="900" height="500" srcset="https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2024/03/The-Football-Cooperative-places-recreational-football-as-a-vehicle-to-promote-physical-activity-and-healthy-ageing-and-positive-mental-health-among-men.jpg 900w, https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2024/03/The-Football-Cooperative-places-recreational-football-as-a-vehicle-to-promote-physical-activity-and-healthy-ageing-and-positive-mental-health-among-men-300x167.jpg 300w, https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2024/03/The-Football-Cooperative-places-recreational-football-as-a-vehicle-to-promote-physical-activity-and-healthy-ageing-and-positive-mental-health-among-men-768x427.jpg 768w" sizes="(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 900px" /></p>
<p>• <strong>Sensational Kids</strong>, which provides timely and affordable therapeutic supports for children with additional needs.</p>
<p><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2024/03/Sensational-Kids-provides-timely-and-affordable-therapeutic-supports-for-children-with-additional-needs-CROPPED.jpg" alt="" width="1811" height="809" srcset="https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2024/03/Sensational-Kids-provides-timely-and-affordable-therapeutic-supports-for-children-with-additional-needs-CROPPED.jpg 1811w, https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2024/03/Sensational-Kids-provides-timely-and-affordable-therapeutic-supports-for-children-with-additional-needs-CROPPED-300x134.jpg 300w, https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2024/03/Sensational-Kids-provides-timely-and-affordable-therapeutic-supports-for-children-with-additional-needs-CROPPED-1024x457.jpg 1024w, https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2024/03/Sensational-Kids-provides-timely-and-affordable-therapeutic-supports-for-children-with-additional-needs-CROPPED-768x343.jpg 768w, https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2024/03/Sensational-Kids-provides-timely-and-affordable-therapeutic-supports-for-children-with-additional-needs-CROPPED-1536x686.jpg 1536w, https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2024/03/Sensational-Kids-provides-timely-and-affordable-therapeutic-supports-for-children-with-additional-needs-CROPPED-1080x482.jpg 1080w" sizes="(max-width: 1811px) 100vw, 1811px" /></p>
<p>• <strong>ClareCare</strong> co-ordinates and provides services for elderly people in Co. Clare.</p>
<p>• <strong>The Together Academy</strong> runs culinary training as part of a two-year employability programme.</p>
<p>• S<strong>aoirse &#8211; Ethnic Hands on Deck</strong> is committed to the economic empowerment of migrant women in Cork.</p>
<p><strong>Changing Ireland spoke to <a href="https://changingireland.ie/49-member-network-has-new-team/">Philip O’Donnell </a>from the Irish Local Development Network (ILDN) at the awards, the overall aim of which was to promote and reward good practice in the monitoring and measurement of social impact.</strong></p>
<h3>4 reasons to measure</h3>
<p>Philip works as the ILDN’s research, policy, and rural affairs officer, and he explained why monitoring and measuring impact has become so important:</p>
<blockquote><p>“Firstly, social enterprises operate in an increasingly competitive environment. Whether they rely predominantly on grant funding or on traded income, invariably there isn&#8217;t enough money to go around, and they need to be able to illustrate the organisation&#8217;s social impact,” he said.</p>
<p>“Secondly, in a world of greenwashing, pinkwashing, etc., the public need to know that they&#8217;re not being hoodwinked by hollow claims. The level of trust in individual social enterprises is generally strong, because these social enterprises tend to be firmly grounded in their communities. However, it is important for the image of the sector as a whole that there is an evidence base to show they are achieving real results.</p>
<p>“Thirdly, social enterprises themselves need to know whether their strategies are working or not.</p>
<p>“Lastly, measuring social impact is a challenge for social enterprises. A survey in Scotland showed it to be the issue of single greatest concern for social enterprises there, and we regularly hear Irish social enterprises voice similar concerns. We hope that this competition will help to demystify measuring social impact, and show that there is no single &#8216;right&#8217; way to measure or monitor impact,” he added.</p></blockquote>
<figure aria-describedby="caption-attachment-253899"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2024/03/At-the-awards-Philip-ODonnell-Martina-Earley-Una-Lowry-Rob-Nicholson-Carol-Baumann-and-Michelle-Mullally-LIGHTLY-CROPPED-scaled.jpg" alt="" width="2560" height="1855" srcset="https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2024/03/At-the-awards-Philip-ODonnell-Martina-Earley-Una-Lowry-Rob-Nicholson-Carol-Baumann-and-Michelle-Mullally-LIGHTLY-CROPPED-scaled.jpg 2560w, https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2024/03/At-the-awards-Philip-ODonnell-Martina-Earley-Una-Lowry-Rob-Nicholson-Carol-Baumann-and-Michelle-Mullally-LIGHTLY-CROPPED-300x217.jpg 300w, https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2024/03/At-the-awards-Philip-ODonnell-Martina-Earley-Una-Lowry-Rob-Nicholson-Carol-Baumann-and-Michelle-Mullally-LIGHTLY-CROPPED-1024x742.jpg 1024w, https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2024/03/At-the-awards-Philip-ODonnell-Martina-Earley-Una-Lowry-Rob-Nicholson-Carol-Baumann-and-Michelle-Mullally-LIGHTLY-CROPPED-768x557.jpg 768w, https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2024/03/At-the-awards-Philip-ODonnell-Martina-Earley-Una-Lowry-Rob-Nicholson-Carol-Baumann-and-Michelle-Mullally-LIGHTLY-CROPPED-1536x1113.jpg 1536w, https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2024/03/At-the-awards-Philip-ODonnell-Martina-Earley-Una-Lowry-Rob-Nicholson-Carol-Baumann-and-Michelle-Mullally-LIGHTLY-CROPPED-2048x1484.jpg 2048w, https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2024/03/At-the-awards-Philip-ODonnell-Martina-Earley-Una-Lowry-Rob-Nicholson-Carol-Baumann-and-Michelle-Mullally-LIGHTLY-CROPPED-1080x783.jpg 1080w" sizes="(max-width: 2560px) 100vw, 2560px" /><figcaption><em>• At the awards &#8211; Philip O&#8217;Donnell, Martina Earley, Una Lowry, Rob Nicholson, Carol Baumann and Michelle Mullally</em></figcaption></figure>
<h3>LDCs will be with you coming up to the deadline</h3>
<figure aria-describedby="caption-attachment-253883"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2024/03/Ellen-Conway-receives-an-award-on-behalf-of-the-Shona-Project-copy-scaled.jpeg" alt="" width="2560" height="1685" srcset="https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2024/03/Ellen-Conway-receives-an-award-on-behalf-of-the-Shona-Project-copy-scaled.jpeg 2560w, https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2024/03/Ellen-Conway-receives-an-award-on-behalf-of-the-Shona-Project-copy-300x197.jpeg 300w, https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2024/03/Ellen-Conway-receives-an-award-on-behalf-of-the-Shona-Project-copy-1024x674.jpeg 1024w, https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2024/03/Ellen-Conway-receives-an-award-on-behalf-of-the-Shona-Project-copy-768x505.jpeg 768w, https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2024/03/Ellen-Conway-receives-an-award-on-behalf-of-the-Shona-Project-copy-1536x1011.jpeg 1536w, https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2024/03/Ellen-Conway-receives-an-award-on-behalf-of-the-Shona-Project-copy-2048x1348.jpeg 2048w, https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2024/03/Ellen-Conway-receives-an-award-on-behalf-of-the-Shona-Project-copy-1080x711.jpeg 1080w" sizes="(max-width: 2560px) 100vw, 2560px" /><figcaption><em>• Martina Earley, ILDN chair, Ellen Conway from the Shona Project, and Rob Nicholson from the Department of Rural and Community Development.</em></figcaption></figure>
<p>Martina Earley, chair of the ILDN, told awardees in Tullamore: “We are fortunate to have so many social enterprises in Ireland and we must support you in every way we can.”</p>
<p>She said that social enterprises rely on “vital” support from each of the 49 Local Development Companies (LDCs) that the ILDN represents.</p>
<blockquote><p>“LDCs are the ones who will be with you coming up to 12 o’clock when you’re trying to get a funding application in,” she remarked.<br />
Martina congratulated the winners: “They provided a compelling account of the collective impact of our social enterprise sector.”<br />
Una Lowry, CEO of Dublin South City Partnership, also spoke.</p>
<p>“Measuring is a luxury for most social enterprises, as they’re sometimes just so busy keeping the doors open. You’ve to be an expert in HR, management, all while running a business, and a lot of them can’t afford the time for measuring social impact,&#8221; she explained.<br />
Una was delighted therefore with the level of interest in the awards – there were 62 applications.</p>
<p>“Dormant Accounts Funding from the Department of Rural and Community Development (DRCD) enabled us to run the competition and publish the toolkit,” she added.</p></blockquote>
<p>The toolkit is available on the ILDN’s website: <a href="https://ildn.ie/wp-content/uploads/2024/02/ILDN-Social-Enterprise-IMPACT-Awards-Booklet.pdf">www.ildn.ie </a> or just click the image below.</p>
<p><a href="https://ildn.ie/wp-content/uploads/2024/02/ILDN-Social-Enterprise-IMPACT-Awards-Booklet.pdf"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2024/03/Social-Enterprise-IMPACT-Awards-tips.png" alt="" width="620" height="877" srcset="https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2024/03/Social-Enterprise-IMPACT-Awards-tips.png 620w, https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2024/03/Social-Enterprise-IMPACT-Awards-tips-212x300.png 212w" sizes="(max-width: 620px) 100vw, 620px" /></a></p>
<blockquote><p><a href="https://changingireland.ie/rob-nicholson-gives-heads-up-on-new-social-enterprise-policy/">Rob Nicholson gives heads up on new social enterprise policy</a></p></blockquote>
<p><iframe sandbox="allow-scripts" security="restricted" title="“Rob Nicholson gives heads up on new social enterprise policy” — Changing Ireland" src="https://changingireland.ie/rob-nicholson-gives-heads-up-on-new-social-enterprise-policy/embed/#?secret=uxXiON4nVa#?secret=XWD3Jqp74j" width="600" height="338" frameborder="0" marginwidth="0" marginheight="0" scrolling="no"></iframe></p>
<blockquote><p><a href="https://changingireland.ie/the-football-co-op-gives-a-e17-60-social-return-on-every-e1-invested/">The Football Co-op gives a €17.60 social return on every €1 invested</a></p></blockquote>
<p><iframe sandbox="allow-scripts" security="restricted" title="“The Football Co-op gives a €17.60 social return on every €1 invested” — Changing Ireland" src="https://changingireland.ie/the-football-co-op-gives-a-e17-60-social-return-on-every-e1-invested/embed/#?secret=cvLlROxZkK#?secret=qvUi9cOVUs" width="600" height="338" frameborder="0" marginwidth="0" marginheight="0" scrolling="no"></iframe></p>
<blockquote><p><a href="https://changingireland.ie/measuring-social-impact-learn-from-those-who-won-e45k/">Measuring social impact: Learn from those who won €45k</a></p></blockquote>
<p><iframe sandbox="allow-scripts" security="restricted" title="“Measuring social impact: Learn from those who won €45k” — Changing Ireland" src="https://changingireland.ie/measuring-social-impact-learn-from-those-who-won-e45k/embed/#?secret=V4y3lLp51n#?secret=OusNDemsbY" width="600" height="338" frameborder="0" marginwidth="0" marginheight="0" scrolling="no"></iframe></p>', 'The national Social Enterprise IMPACT Awards ceremony, held on February 20, in Tullamore, Co. Offaly, took place amidst a rise globally in greenwashing and pinkwashing, where the public need reassurance and social enterprises need to show they are hitting their targets.', 'https://changingireland.ie/wp-content/uploads/2024/03/And-the-6-winners-are.png', '', '[{"slug":"enterprise","title":"Enterprise"}]', '[]', '', 253867, 1, '2024-03-07T13:49:14Z', '2024-03-20T11:49:10Z', '2024-03-07T13:49:14Z')
